{{wikipedia|Takbir}}

===Alternative forms===
[[Image:Flag of Waziristan resistance (1930s).svg|thumb|Flag of 1930s [[Waziristan|Waziristan (Pakistan)]] resistance movement: {{lang|ar|الله أكبر}}]]
[[Image:Flag of Iraq.svg|thumb|Flag of Iraq: {{lang|ar|اللّٰه أكبر}}]]
* {{l|en|Allah akbar}}
* {{l|en|Ola hu Uber}} (India, slang, derogatory)

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ar|ء ل ه|ك ب ر}}
From {{bor|en|ar|اللّٰهُ أَكْبَر}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˌaləhuː ˈakbɑː/|/əˌlɑːhuː ˈakbɑː/}}

===Interjection===
{{en-interj|head=Allahu akbar}}

# {{lb|en|Islam}} "[[God]] is most [[great]]"; the [[Muslim]] [[takbir]], or liturgical [[proclamation]], used to accept sovereignty of God and as a common form of thanksgiving. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* '''1742''', James Fraser, ''The History of Nadir Shah'', London 1742, p. 126n:
#*: ''Tokbîr'' is repeating three times these Words, {{lang|ar|اللّٰه أكبر}} '''''Allah Akbar''''', which is to say, GOD ''is Greatest'', before the ''Kelmah'' abovementioned.
#* '''2004''', Yuri Bagrov, ''The Guardian'', 22 Jun 2004:
#*: The official said it was not immediately clear who the attackers were, but said some of them were shouting "'''Allahu Akbar'''" - a frequent cry of Chechnya's separatist rebels.

